<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kakatiya dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">1163 CE – 1323 CE · capital Orugallu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Telugu dynasty of Warangal that united the Andhra country, built the Thousand Pillar Temple and was famed for its diamonds and tanks.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Kakatiyas began as feudatories of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/western-chalukya-empire">Chalukyas of Kalyani</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telangana">Telangana</a> region. As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukya</a> power declined, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prola-ii">Prola II</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudradeva-kakatiya">Rudradeva</a> asserted independence; Rudradeva declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> around 1163, built the Thousand Pillar Temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanamkonda">Hanamkonda</a> and laid out a new capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warangal">Orugallu</a> (Warangal).</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganapati-kakatiya">Ganapatideva</a> united most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telugu-language">Telugu</a>-speaking lands and encouraged trade through the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/motupalli">Motupalli</a>. His daughter Rudramadevi ruled as one of the few reigning queens of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, praised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo">Marco Polo</a>. The kingdom built large irrigation tanks such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramappa-temple">Ramappa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakhal-lake">Pakhal</a>, and the Ramappa temple is now a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayaka">Nayaka</a> military chiefs held land in return for service.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warangal">Warangal</a> repeatedly from 1309. In 1323 an army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-bin-tughluq">Ulugh Khan</a>, the future Muhammad bin Tughluq, captured the city and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prataparudra-kakatiya">Prataparudra</a> prisoner. Former Kakatiya <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayaka">Nayakas</a> soon led a revolt, founding the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/musunuri-nayaks">Musunuri Nayaks</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/recherla-nayakas">Recherla Nayakas</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kakatiya-dynasty.jpg" alt="Kakatiya dynasty" width="320" height="296"><figcaption>Image: Map created from DEMIS Mapserver, which are public domain. Koba-chan. Reference: [1] · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Kakatiyas.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1163 CE – 1323 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Orugallu</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ganapatideva, Rudramadevi, Prataparudra</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Telugu, Sanskrit</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kakatiyas, Kakatiyas of Warangal</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1243&amp;focus=kakatiya-dynasty" aria-label="Kakatiya dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6839 1511 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kakatiya dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7159 1767L7088 1770M7159 1767L7018 1385M7159 1767L7159 1767M7159 1767L7197 1830"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7088 1770h0M7018 1385h0M7159 1767h0M7197 1830h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7159 1767h0